<Legal
  title="Accessibility"
  description="Our accessibility commitment for this site and our products: what we have done, what we have not yet verified, and how to tell us about a barrier."
  eyebrow="Trust"
  intro="People who are unwell, tired, distracted or in distress are exactly who our products are for. An interface they cannot use is not a minor defect."
  updated="2026-11-09"
  scopeNote="We aim to meet WCAG 2.2 Level AA. We have not yet commissioned an independent accessibility audit of this site, so this page states what we have built and tested ourselves — not a certified conformance claim."
>
  <h2>What we have built into this site</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Keyboard access throughout.</strong> Every interactive element can be
      reached and operated with a keyboard, in a logical order, with a visible focus
      indicator that meets contrast requirements. A "skip to main content" link is
      the first thing a keyboard user reaches.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Semantic structure.</strong> Pages use real headings in order, real
      lists, real landmarks and real buttons and links — a link navigates, a button
      acts, and neither is faked with a styled div.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Text alternatives.</strong> Every meaningful image carries a text alternative.
      Decorative graphics are hidden from assistive technology rather than announced
      as noise.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reduced motion.</strong> The site has a small amount of ambient animation.
      If your system asks for reduced motion, all of it stops.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Text scaling and reflow.</strong> Type is set in relative units and
      layouts reflow, so enlarging text does not clip content or force horizontal
      scrolling.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No motion or colour as the only signal.</strong> Meaning is carried
      by text as well as colour, so nothing depends on seeing the difference between
      mint and lime.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we have not verified</h2>
  <p>
    We want to be straightforward about the limits of the above. We have not
    yet:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Commissioned an independent accessibility audit of this site.</li>
    <li>
      Completed testing with screen-reader users, rather than testing with a
      screen reader ourselves.
    </li>
    <li>
      Tested against the full range of assistive technologies, including switch
      control and voice control.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Until those are done, treat this page as a statement of intent and effort,
    not as a conformance claim.
  </p>

  <h2>Accessibility in the Tether Health app</h2>
  <p>
    Every button and control in the app has a label that screen readers such as
    VoiceOver read aloud, screen titles are marked as headings, and charts and
    timers are read as words. Body text follows your phone's text size; we check
    layouts at 200% and 300%, and a few compact controls still shorten their
    labels at the largest sizes. Zones, alerts and selections are shown with
    words or symbols, not colour alone. When your phone asks for reduced motion,
    the breathing ring and loading placeholders stand still and pages cross-fade
    instead of sliding. The app has no audio or video content.
  </p>
  <p>
    What the app does not do yet: it has no dark appearance, and it does not
    follow the phone's increased-contrast setting, although its text colours are
    chosen to meet WCAG AA contrast. We are working on these. Our checks so far
    are automated; screen-reader and voice-control users have not yet tested the
    app on a phone, and there has been no independent audit.
  </p>

  <h2>Tell us about a barrier</h2>
  <p>
    If any part of this site or our products is difficult or impossible for you
    to use, please tell us at <SupportEmail />. It helps if you can say what you
    were trying to do, what happened, and what you were using — but please write
    to us even if you would rather not go into detail.
  </p>
  <p>
    We treat accessibility reports as defects, not as feature requests, and we
    will tell you what we are doing about yours.
  </p>
</Legal>
